  "notes": "Post-disco funk-pop with a conversational, close-mic'd delivery. The documented production method is loop-first — \"the main guitar riff and drums on Espresso are Splice sample-pack loops, then Bunetta plays live bass, drums, guitar, keys and percussion over them\" — so the model is a sampled grid with played layers on top: `quantizeStrength` stays high but `velocityVar` and the perc jitter are live-player numbers. \"A four-square backbeat with a real-kit feel, tambourine on offbeats, and no trap hat vocabulary\" is authored literally: kick on 1 and 3 with a low syncopation, `tambourine` at `offbeat` placement, and `rolls.freqPerBar` 0 — the absence of the roll vocabulary is a real delta against a parent whose rolls run at 0.2. The bass is the second hook: \"played funk/disco electric bass on syncopated 16ths, walking between chord tones, sitting loud in the mix\", which is `independent_riff` at 0.34 with walking cell shapes, and there is deliberately no `drums.bass808` block. Harmony is \"two- or four-chord vamps with 7ths\" over \"dominant-7th and major-6th colour over a static tonic\", so the families are short, `harmonicRhythm` leans `vamp`, and `triad` drops. Melody encodes \"short, punchline-shaped phrases with speech rhythm and a small interval span, ending on a spoken aside\" as a 1—2 bar phrase, a tight `motifPitchCount` and `endVariation`; \"backing vocals are close unison stacks rather than wide harmony\" is the low counter density. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum probability, the humanize numbers, the scale weighting and the register bands; the research states the method and the palette but gives no numbers, and its key/BPM band is marked genre-derived. `bpm.mode` 105 is the midpoint of the stated 100—110 core. ⚠ The entry's [LOW-CONF] mark on the vocal-placement claim is repeated here as the reason the melody register is a reading rather than a measurement.",
